{"file":{"content":"**Finding (the finiteness leak, residue-only):** the annealed prediction that the\ngap closes at low frequency is **refuted** — the exact residue operator keeps a\nuniform gap bounded away from 0 as Q→∞ over the tested range (3²→3⁷). The\n\"finiteness leak\" is not realized for the residue operator. Reconciles with the\nshadow analysis: the residue-only operator is the right object, and it gaps\nevery sector; the killed-height κ_Q=0 is a height-coupling artifact.\n\n(Annealed vs exact, as before: `corr(exact_s0, annealed) = 0.38`, mean\ndeviation 0.25 — the exact gap is not the i.i.d. Fourier value, here decisively\n*larger* at low frequency.)\n\n## F — exact gap vs annealed Fourier (Q=27/81/243/729, Bmax=28, 48 sectors)\nPre-registered: compare exact `ρ` to `|p̂(ξ)| = 1/√(5−4cos 2πξ)`.\n\n- **mean |exact_s0 − annealed| = 0.254**; **corr(exact_s0, annealed) = 0.38**.\n  The exact coupled operator only weakly tracks the i.i.d. Fourier value and\n  deviates by ±0.1–0.36 per sector. `rho_annealed` and `rho_exact` are reported\n  in separate columns and are not interchangeable.\n\n**Finding:** the i.i.d. Fourier shadow is *not* the exact gap; κ_Q must be\nmeasured from the coupled operator (the \"finiteness leak\" is real). Reconciles\nwith E, where κ_Q is sizeable and Q-stable.","filePath":"/home/john/mynotes/deterministic_equidistribution_theory/c_suite/VALIDATION_REPORT.md","numLines":22,"startLine":112,"totalLines":164},"type":"text"}